{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2715","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2715","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2715\",\"parent_revision\":\"0a0fbff0139b29ae9c64f43d788b35207e7d7bbee38e8932da3c4d2a15656f87\",\"root_revision\":\"0a0fbff0139b29ae9c64f43d788b35207e7d7bbee38e8932da3c4d2a15656f87\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nA behavior change to `reduce.py`'s `normalize_location()` altered corroboration-key behavior for a location format used by an external, cross-plugin consumer at `examples/solid-review-ab/runner/scorer.py`. That consumer's own tests and docstrings still describe the pre-change behavior, and none of its existing tests exercise the changed code path.\\n\\nWhere observed: PR #2626 code review, `examples/solid-review-ab/`.\\n\\nImpact: currently latent (the consumer's existing test data doesn't trigger the changed path), but the external contract is now undocumented drift with a false-green test suite \u2014 any future data matching the changed pattern will behave differently than the consumer's own tests/docs claim, with nothing to catch it.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T06:00:27.283386Z</sub>\\n\\nCLAIM 1: \\\"scorer.py's normalize_location docstring does not document how heading anchors (path.md#heading:N) are handled\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  Source: /home/user/claude_skills/examples/solid-review-ab/runner/scorer.py lines 97-117\\n  Retrieved: 2026-07-06\\n  Content: |\\n    The scorer.py docstring states:\\n    \\\"Delegates to reduce.py's normalize_location (which strips a leading /)\\n    then strips any leading ../ segments... Returns: Normalised repo-relative path:line string.\\\"\\n    No mention of heading anchor handling (e.g., path.md#heading:N).\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  Source: /home/user/claude_skills/plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/reduce.py lines 81-97\\n  Finding: reduce.py's docstring also omits heading anchor documentation; the regex LOCATION_LINE_RE = r\\\"(\\\\S+):(\\\\d+)\\\" captures path and line number but preserves any \\\"#\\\" anchors as part of the path component without explicit documentation.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The docstrings describe only \\\"/\\\" and \\\"../\\\" prefix stripping, not heading anchor preservation. A caller cannot determine from the documentation that heading anchors are preserved (they pass through as part of the path component matched by the regex).\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM 2: \\\"test_scorer.py and test_scorer_independent.py do not test normalize_location with heading-anchor inputs (path.md#heading:N) or no-line-number inputs\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  Source: /home/user/claude_skills/examples/solid-review-ab/tests/test_scorer.py lines 88-121 (all normalize_location tests) and test_scorer_independent.py lines 312-361 (all normalize_location tests)\\n  Retrieved: 2026-07-06\\n  Content: |\\n    test_scorer.py tests:\\n    - test_normalize_location_strips_leading_slash: \\\"/corpus/cases/01.py:17\\\"\\n    - test_normalize_location_preserves_directory: \\\"src/foo/config.py:10\\\" and \\\"tests/foo/config.py:10\\\"\\n    - test_normalize_location_trims_whitespace: \\\"  corpus/cases/01.py:17  \\\"\\n    - test_normalize_location_no_line_returns_stripped: \\\"  some/path/file.py  \\\" (no line number case present)\\n    \\n    test_scorer_independent.py tests:\\n    - test_normalize_location_absolute_path_equals_relative_path: \\\"/corpus/cases/01_srp_god_object.py:12\\\" and \\\"corpus/cases/01_srp_god_object.py:12\\\"\\n    - test_normalize_location_same_basename_different_dirs_remain_distinct: \\\"src/foo/config.py:10\\\" and \\\"tests/foo/config.py:10\\\"\\n    - test_normalize_location_whitespace_is_trimmed: \\\"  corpus/cases/02_ocp.py:25  \\\"\\n    \\n    Zero tests for heading anchors (e.g., \\\"corpus/cases/file.md#heading:10\\\") in either file.\\n    No no-line-number test in test_scorer_independent.py.\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  Source: grep -n \\\"anchor\\\\|#\\\\|heading\\\" across both test files\\n  Finding: No matches; the test suites are comprehensive for /, ../, and whitespace edge cases but lack anchor and no-line-number coverage in test_scorer_independent.py.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: test_scorer.py does include the no-line-number case (line 117-121), but test_scorer_independent.py omits it. Neither file tests heading anchor preservation, which is a behavioral feature of the regex-based implementation.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM 3: \\\"corpus/gold.json location entries use only repo-relative path:line format with no heading anchors\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  Source: /home/user/claude_skills/examples/solid-review-ab/corpus/gold.json lines 1-217 (all 26 location entries)\\n  Retrieved: 2026-07-06\\n  Content: |\\n    All location entries follow the pattern \\\"corpus/cases/FILENAME.py:LINE\\\":\\n    - \\\"location\\\": \\\"corpus/cases/01_srp_god_object.py:17\\\"\\n    - \\\"location\\\": \\\"corpus/cases/01_srp_god_object.py:57\\\"\\n    - \\\"location\\\": \\\"corpus/cases/02_ocp_type_dispatch.py:19\\\"\\n    ... (24 additional entries, all following same pattern)\\n    \\n    No entry contains '#' or heading anchor syntax.\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  Source: Manual scan + grep -E '#' against the JSON file\\n  Finding: Zero matches for the '#' character in any location field.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: Every location in the gold set uses the normalized format that reduce.py and scorer.py produce: repo-relative path (without leading '/'), followed by ':', followed by line number. No heading anchors are present in the corpus.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM 4: \\\"scorer.py's normalize_location produces different output from reduce.py's normalize_location for ../ prefixed inputs\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  Source: /home/user/claude_skills/plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/reduce.py lines 81-97 and /home/user/claude_skills/examples/solid-review-ab/runner/scorer.py lines 97-117\\n  Retrieved: 2026-07-06\\n  Code path for input \\\"../corpus/cases/file.py:10\\\":\\n  \\n  reduce.py:\\n    - LOCATION_LINE_RE.search() finds \\\":10\\\"\\n    - group(1) = \\\"../corpus/cases/file.py\\\"\\n    - path = \\\"../corpus/cases/file.py\\\".lstrip(\\\"/\\\") = \\\"../corpus/cases/file.py\\\" (lstrip removes '/' only, not '..')\\n    - Returns: \\\"../corpus/cases/file.py:10\\\"\\n  \\n  scorer.py:\\n    - result = reduce.normalize_location(\\\"../corpus/cases/file.py:10\\\") = \\\"../corpus/cases/file.py:10\\\"\\n    - while result.startswith(\\\"../\\\"): result = result[3:]\\n    - First iteration: result = \\\"corpus/cases/file.py:10\\\"\\n    - Condition false, exit\\n    - Returns: \\\"corpus/cases/file.py:10\\\"\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  Source: Code inspection confirms lstrip(\\\"/\\\") does not remove \\\"..\\\" prefix (only leading '/' characters)\\n  Finding: reduce.py preserves \\\"../\\\" in the path; scorer.py strips it.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: For any input with leading \\\"../\\\", reduce.py returns the path with \\\"../\\\" intact, while scorer.py's wrapper removes all leading \\\"../\\\" segments. They produce semantically different normalized outputs.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM 5: \\\"normalize_location() is identical in main branch and pr-2626 branch\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  Source: git show main:plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/reduce.py lines 81-97\\n  Source: git show pr-2626:plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/reduce.py lines 81-97\\n  Retrieved: 2026-07-06\\n  Content: |\\n    main:\\n    ```python\\n    def normalize_location(raw: str) -> str:\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Normalize a location to `path:line`...\\n        match = LOCATION_LINE_RE.search(raw)\\n        if match:\\n            path = match.group(1).lstrip(\\\"/\\\")\\n            return f\\\"{path}:{match.group(2)}\\\"\\n        return raw.strip()\\n    ```\\n    \\n    pr-2626:\\n    ```python\\n    def normalize_location(raw: str) -> str:\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Normalize a location to `path:line`...\\n        match = LOCATION_LINE_RE.search(raw)\\n        if match:\\n            path = match.group(1).lstrip(\\\"/\\\")\\n            return f\\\"{path}:{match.group(2)}\\\"\\n        return raw.strip()\\n    ```\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  Source: diff <(git show main:...) <(git show pr-2626:...) \u2014 exit code 0 (no differences)\\n  Finding: Byte-level diff confirms identical implementation.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The function definition, docstring, logic, and whitespace are identical across both branches. No drift has occurred.\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:58:25.390636Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: fix: reduce.py change drifted an external consumer's (solid-review-ab) contract and test coverage\\nDate: 2026-07-06\\nGoal: Identify the specific location format where scorer.py's normalize_location wrapper contract/tests diverge from reduce.py's actual behavior, and ensure documentation and tests accurately represent the full input surface.\\nConditions:\\n1. reduce.py's normalize_location() current behavior | Status: AVAILABLE \u2014 LOCATION_LINE_RE = r\\\"(\\\\S+):(\\\\d+)\\\"; strips leading / from path group; heading anchors preserved (file.md#heading:10 \u2192 file.md#heading:10); fallback: raw.strip() for inputs without a line number\\n2. scorer.py's normalize_location wrapper | Status: AVAILABLE \u2014 lines 97-117: delegates to _reduce.normalize_location(raw) then strips any leading ../ segments; imported via importlib from _REDUCE_PATH; docstring says \\\"reduce.py strips a leading /\\\" only; ../ stripping is scorer-layer addition; heading-anchor and no-line-number behavior not documented\\n3. Whether normalize_location() changed or was newly created | Status: AVAILABLE \u2014 fact-checker for #2713 confirmed: file created 2026-06-07 as a NEW file; no prior versions in git history; no behavioral change from a prior version exists\\n4. Whether reduce.py's normalize_location() differs between main and pr-2626 | Status: AVAILABLE \u2014 pr-2626 branch reduce.py normalize_location() is identical to main (read both); the \\\"behavior change\\\" referenced in the issue description is the function's original introduction, not a subsequent modification\\n5. What specific location formats are NOT covered by scorer.py's existing tests | Status: DERIVABLE \u2014 read test_scorer.py and test_scorer_independent.py fixtures to identify which normalize_location input patterns are absent; known gap: heading-anchor inputs (path.md#anchor:N), no-line-number inputs (path/to/file.md)\\n6. What scorer.py's test docstrings claim about normalize_location behavior | Status: DERIVABLE \u2014 read test file docstrings and assert descriptions to identify which behaviors are asserted as expected\\n7. Whether the drift is documentation-only or requires a code change to scorer.py | Status: DERIVABLE \u2014 depends on whether scorer.py's wrapper behavior for uncovered inputs is correct (doc/test fix only) or incorrect (code change required)\\n8. Downstream impact on gold.json scoring and P/R/F1 metrics | Status: DERIVABLE \u2014 read corpus/gold.json location format to confirm whether any gold-set location uses heading anchors or no-line-number format that would trigger the uncovered paths\\nAVAILABLE count: 4\\nDERIVABLE count: 4\\nMISSING count: 0\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **reduce.py change drifted an external consumer's (solid-review-ab) contract and test coverage** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Agent task - auto-derived from /code-review max --fix pr session, PR #2626\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-07-03\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-07-06)\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `examples/solid-review-ab/.claude/agent-memory/python-engineering-python-pytest-architect/project_scorer_independence.md` - Provides routing guidance for normalize_location test authorship. Missing heading-anchor case means automated agents will not add this test case when authoring new tests for this module. The note \\\"do NOT test slash stripping on bare paths\\\" is correct but leaves the corresponding \\\"DO test heading-anchor\\\" guidance absent. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Agent-authored tests will follow this memory and perpetuate the coverage gap. The memory file functions as the specification for what to test \u2014 an incomplete specification produces incomplete tests.\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n\\n- `examples/solid-review-ab/runner/scorer.py::normalize_location` - Thin wrapper around `_reduce.normalize_location` that additionally strips leading `../` segments. Loaded via importlib at lines 46-87. Docstring (lines 97-117) documents only the leading `/` strip \u2014 omits heading-anchor preservation and no-line-number fallback behaviors entirely. The `../` stripping layer adds a fourth path variant not covered by any test. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Incomplete docstring creates false specification for future callers; heading-anchor and no-line-number contract claims are absent; any future data in those formats will behave differently than the docstring implies.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n\\n- `examples/solid-review-ab/runner/scorer.py::reported_keys` - Calls `normalize_location` indirectly via `parse_report`/`Finding.location` pipeline at scoring time. If heading-anchor or no-line-number inputs reach `reported_keys`, the normalized output feeds into the `(group, location)` tuple that is compared against `gold.json`. Correctness of this comparison depends on consistent normalize behavior. | Risk: LOW | Why: Gold.json currently contains only standard `path:line` format; no heading-anchor or no-line-number entries exist today, making the risk latent rather than active.\\n\\n- `examples/solid-review-ab/corpus/gold.json` - Ground truth for all 20+ (group, location) scoring targets. All entries use standard `corpus/cases/filename.py:line` format. No heading anchors or bare paths present. | Risk: LOW | Why: Current format is safe; risk activates only if future gold entries or arm outputs use heading-anchor or no-line-number forms. Confirmed false-green vector: tests pass because the data never exercises the undocumented paths.\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/reduce.py::normalize_location` - Sole producer of the `normalize_location` contract. Function handles three distinct input branches: (1) standard `path:line` \u2192 strips leading `/`, returns `path:line`; (2) heading-anchor format `file.md#heading:10` \u2192 same regex path, anchor preserved in group(1); (3) no-line-number input \u2192 `raw.strip()` fallback. All three behaviors originate here and must stay in sync with scorer.py's wrapper docstring and both test files. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Two of the three behaviors (heading anchor, no-line fallback) are either undocumented or incompletely documented in the consumer's wrapper. Any future change to the regex or fallback will not be caught by CI because `test_reduce.py` is outside `testpaths`.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\n- `pyproject.toml` `[tool.pytest.ini_options]` `testpaths` - Includes `examples/solid-review-ab/tests` (line 451) but does NOT include `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/` where `test_reduce.py` lives. Also includes `./examples/solid-review-ab` in `pythonpath` (line 439), enabling the `runner` package import. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Exclusion of the producer test file from `testpaths` is the structural root cause of the CI gap. Changing `normalize_location` in `reduce.py` without running `test_reduce.py` will produce no CI failure warning. This configuration produces a structurally false-green test suite.\\n\\n- `pyproject.toml` `[tool.coverage.run]` `source` - Set to `[\\\"**/scripts\\\"]`. `reduce.py` is in a `scripts/` subdirectory, so it is included in the coverage source. However, the tests exercising `reduce.py` (`test_reduce.py`) are excluded from `testpaths`, meaning coverage data for `normalize_location` in `reduce.py` is only produced via scorer tests calling through the importlib bridge \u2014 not via direct producer tests. | Risk: LOW | Why: Coverage reports will show reduce.py coverage, but that coverage comes from scorer tests that don't exercise heading-anchor inputs, creating a misleadingly green coverage report.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `examples/solid-review-ab/runner/scorer.py` docstring at lines 97-117 - States \\\"reduce.py strips a leading /\\\" as the complete description of normalization. Does not document: (a) heading-anchor inputs are passed through with anchor preserved; (b) no-line-number inputs receive `raw.strip()` and are returned unchanged (no `/` strip); (c) the scorer layer additionally strips `../` prefixes. A developer reading only this docstring would have an incomplete and partially inaccurate mental model of the contract. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Docstring is the primary behavioral specification for callers. Omitting two of the four normalization behaviors means callers may make incorrect assumptions that only break on edge-case inputs.\\n\\n- `examples/solid-review-ab/README.md` lines 81-85 - Documents the `location` field as `path:line` format (`corpus/cases/01_srp_god_object.py:12`). States \\\"Normalization for matching MUST match reduce.py's `normalize_location` (strip a leading `/`, trim whitespace, PRESERVE the directory)\\\". Does not mention heading-anchor handling or no-line-number fallback as distinct behaviors. | Risk: LOW | Why: README instructs arm agents on what to emit, not what to parse. However if an arm emits heading-anchor locations (e.g. from markdown file review), the documentation gives no guidance on how the scorer will normalize them.\\n\\n- `examples/solid-review-ab/.claude/agent-memory/python-engineering-python-pytest-architect/project_scorer_independence.md` - Agent memory file documenting normalize_location edge cases at lines 19-23. Documents: leading `/` stripping, no-line-number raw.strip() behavior, same-basename directory distinction. Does NOT document heading-anchor behavior. Also states \\\"do NOT test slash stripping on bare paths\\\" which is guidance that should be accompanied by \\\"DO test heading-anchor preservation\\\". | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: This is an AI agent instruction file. Future pytest-architect agents relying on this memory will author tests without heading-anchor coverage, perpetuating the gap through automated agent sessions.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked\\n</div>\\n\\n### Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T06:02:11.837657Z</sub>\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T06:00:50.597806Z</sub>\\n\\n**Type**: missing-guardrail\\n\\n**Rationale**: The system behaved as designed \u2014 code works correctly and tests pass. However, the design itself lacks required validation gates: (1) no rule ensuring docstrings document all supported input formats, (2) no test-coverage audit requiring test inputs to match documented formats, (3) no contract-documentation standard for cross-plugin function wrappers. The function was newly created without a complete contract, and no validation gate caught the incomplete documentation or test coverage. The fix requires adding these quality gates (documentation standards, coverage validation, wrapper contract spec), not changing code logic.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: N/A \u2014 missing-guardrail classification requires no root-cause analysis\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: External consumer (scorer.py) tests pass despite incomplete coverage of normalize_location input formats (heading anchors, no-line-number inputs) \u2192 All normalize_location input formats are explicitly documented in docstrings; test suite covers all documented formats or explicitly marks patterns as out-of-scope; quality gate enforces documentation/test alignment\\n</div>\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: MEDIUM\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `pyproject.toml` testpaths \u2014 excludes `test_reduce.py` from CI; produce-level normalize_location tests are invisible to the test suite; structural root cause of the false-green state\\n  - `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/test_reduce.py` \u2014 producer tests that exist but don't run in CI; no heading-anchor test case present even manually\\n  - `examples/solid-review-ab/.claude/agent-memory/.../project_scorer_independence.md` \u2014 agent instruction file that will propagate the test gap to future automated sessions\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - verification (heading-anchor format untested in all test files)\\n  - documentation (scorer.py docstring documents 1 of 4 normalization behaviors)\\n  - operational (test_reduce.py outside testpaths creates structural CI blind spot)\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - The `testpaths` gap in `pyproject.toml` is a broader infrastructure concern: any other standalone PEP 723 test scripts in `plugins/` that are not in `testpaths` share this isolation pattern. This was not in the original 